# Project Guidelines

## Code Style
- Use TypeScript with React; .tsx for components, .ts for utilities.
- Functional components with hooks; no class components.
- Use Tailwind CSS for styling; apply classes directly in JSX.
- Follow standard React patterns for state and props; keep components focused and reusable.
- Don't use TypeScript's `any` type; prefer specific types or generics for better type safety.
- For React components, the top-level returned JSX tag should start on the same line as the `return` statement, without unnecessary parentheses around the JSX (e.g. `return <div...`).

## Architecture
- Keep the code Next.js convertible; avoid any features or architectural style that are incompatible with Next.js.

## Commit Guidelines
- Keep commit messages lower case and concise, ideally under 50 characters.
- Use the present tense for commit messages (e.g., "add feature" instead of "added feature").

## Knowledge Tracking Rule
- Monitor our conversation for technical gaps or "how does this work" questions.
- Maintain a file at the root called `LEARNING_DEBT.md`.
- When a new concept is introduced, add it to `LEARNING_DEBT.md` with:
  1. The Name of the concept.
  2. A "Plain English" definition.
  3. A code snippet showing how it's used in THIS project.
- After adding an entry, say so in your reply (e.g. "Added to LEARNING_DEBT.md.") so the user can see it was done.